## Lore

The Cyber-Specters are the last real counterculture in Melbourne and Perth:
thousands of hackers who may never see one another''s faces, talking over
the fiber lines and meeting in virtual space. The Central Computer can
trace network activity, but a Specter moves through the lines without being
billed, pinned down or incriminated, like a ghost. Most are passive users
after fun. The O.C.C. is the hard core: someone who enjoys dodging
authority, breaks encryption, steals data and writes code the way a painter
uses a brush.

The authorities paint the movement as a plot against the Administration.
Mostly it is curiosity. A Specter can get into the Central Computer''s data
stores but not the Computer itself, and cannot seize life support, power or
military systems. The careful ones do not rob banks either, since that gets
a hacker caught. What they do is meddle: alter a manifest, delay a
diplomat''s car, leak a hidden plan. Many also want the public to learn what
the Administration hides, including how shaky the Cities'' economies are.

They live double lives, hacking at night and holding an ordinary job by day
on stimulants, hiding the hobby from friends and family for everyone''s
safety. Most are between 15 and 24. They trade stolen data and new
techniques in private homes and small clubs, and some drift into contact
with City Raiders, outcasts, criminals, mutants and outsiders.

## GM Notes

Computer Hacking is stored at 40% (+5% per level). The special ability
gives that total; the skill list prints a bonus of +30%, which would make
it 45%. The G.M. decides which to use.

None of the hacking modifiers or the trace rules are applied by the sheet;
see the two special abilities.

Gadgets: the character owns 1D4+1 from this list. 1 window trembler, 2 line
tap, 3 spy camera, 4 white noise generator, 5 voice scrambler, 6 hand-held
digital voice recorder, 7 audio bug and scanner with a 1 mile (1.6 km)
range, 8 bug detector, 9 portable chip/disk reader, 10 hand-held computer
memory unit.

Also not listed by the sheet: a wristwatch, a packet of Everwake
stimulants, painkillers, and several (quite illegal) books on computer code
and security. The one small firearm comes with two reloads. At the G.M.''s
option the character owns a light anti-assassin M.D.C. vest. The character
may also have a simple ground vehicle (conventional car, motorcycle or
hover car, 150 M.D.C., 220 mph/352 km, no weapons) and a two room
apartment.

Money: savings of 2D4x100 dollars. A mundane day job pays about 1D4x1,000 a
month; a computer or technology job pays 2-5 times that but demands far
more time and accountability. Australian dollars equal credits.

Cybernetics: none to start; a Universal Headjack and ear implant plus 1D4
minor implants are common later. No bionics.

A Psi-Ghost or Psi-Tech (Rifts World Book 12: Psyscape) living in the City
keeps the powers of that R.C.C. and takes this class''s skills and
background, with O.C.C. Related and Secondary skills cut in half.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'cyber-specter');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'cyber-specter';

